1
00:26:37,550 --> 00:26:50,130
What a difference it has made! Nowadays there is no such teaching. The teaching we had here has come to an end — all that is left is sitting in qawwali.

2
00:26:50,970 --> 00:27:01,510
Many people do zikr. In zikr one draws closer to Allah; one remembers Allah, and the thought stays turned towards Allah.

3
00:27:02,890 --> 00:27:06,970
But it stays far off. Only qawwali — they listen to qawwali the whole night.

4
00:27:08,410 --> 00:27:16,590
In it is the mention of the Prophet ﷺ, his glory and greatness; the love for the Prophet ﷺ is intense.

5
00:27:17,910 --> 00:27:23,230
That is necessary in its place. But if that is all there is, how far does one get?

6
00:27:24,210 --> 00:27:35,770
Some elders have given this mark of a good murshid: in whose company all whisperings and idle thoughts stop, and a person's mind becomes bound to Allah and His Messenger ﷺ.

7
00:27:38,090 --> 00:27:45,630
Look first and understand, then give your hand. Otherwise regret follows.

8
00:27:46,610 --> 00:27:53,330
The one who frees you from bondage to everything else — his company is necessary.

9
00:27:54,710 --> 00:28:01,090
If he turns your thoughts towards Allah and His Messenger ﷺ, that is the real thing.

10
00:28:02,010 --> 00:28:05,810
To see good and bad for what they are is the eye of a great one.

11
00:28:07,570 --> 00:28:30,730
Now see, what do I always tell you all? When I speak, keep looking into my eyes. Some people sit with their heads down while I talk, so I tell them at once: look into my eyes.

12
00:28:32,570 --> 00:28:47,230
The states that come out of my eyes come into your eyes too. That is why you should keep your eyes open and keep looking at the murshid.

13
00:28:48,370 --> 00:28:53,670
The Companions used to keep looking at the Prophet ﷺ — and they also lowered their gaze out of respect.

14
00:28:53,670 --> 00:28:59,550
To see good and bad for what they are is the eye of a great one.

15
00:29:00,510 --> 00:29:10,410
When the heart is straight, the eyes stay fixed on one point; the eye of a troubled person stays troubled too.

16
00:29:11,890 --> 00:29:21,470
Some say that if you look at a wali from behind, he often turns round and looks; and if you ask him something, he answers at once.

17
00:29:22,390 --> 00:29:39,830
There is an incident. The Chadarghat bridge, towards Malakpet — after crossing the bridge, on the way to Sultan Bazar, there is a mosque on the left-hand side.

18
00:29:40,750 --> 00:30:02,830
In that mosque people were sitting for namaz and talking about something. An old man, a wali, was sitting there, and everyone was listening to him.

19
00:30:04,370 --> 00:30:33,830
It so happened that Hazrat Qadeer Piya Sarkar was entering the mosque by the door. As he stepped onto the stairs, the old wali threw his staff and said: 'If anyone has the strength, let him pick it up!'

20
00:30:36,160 --> 00:30:41,900
Qadeer Piya Sarkar caught it. 'Oh — it is you who has come!' He had picked it up.

21
00:30:42,760 --> 00:31:01,620
Why? Without turning round, the old wali knew that a wali was behind him — that is why he threw his staff. 'Oh, it is you! How else would I know you had come?'

22
00:31:10,740 --> 00:31:39,600
Did you see? It says here: a wali recognises a wali. If a wali is behind him, he turns and looks — he knows someone is there.

23
00:31:40,380 --> 00:32:25,990
Remember: the thoughts of every person and the effect of his company draw others in and spread all around — like a stone thrown into water: the ripples come.

24
00:32:27,870 --> 00:32:48,710
Good company leads to good, and bad company leads to evil. Throw one pebble into water and it makes ripples; a circle forms.

25
00:32:50,670 --> 00:33:16,330
Good company leads to good, bad company to evil. But if a big stone and a small pebble are thrown into the water together, the big stone's ripples swallow the pebble's ripples.

26
00:33:19,330 --> 00:33:29,550
Do you understand? The small pebble's ripples are gone; the big stone's are at work — they overpower them.

27
00:33:31,430 --> 00:33:52,830
In the same way, a person of ordinary thoughts and weak faith who sits in the company of a perfected one — his doubtful and harmful thoughts grow weaker and weaker.

28
00:33:53,470 --> 00:34:11,210
He gains the strength to leave his weak thoughts; all the bad thoughts fade away, and he is shaped by the company of the perfected, strong-willed one.

29
00:38:46,270 --> 00:38:56,730
If someone makes a mistake after giving bai'at, he should repent and say: Astaghfirullah al-'Azeem alladhi la ilaha illa Huwa al-Hayy al-Qayyum wa atubu ilayh. Recite this much.

30
00:38:58,770 --> 00:39:13,470
Even if a sinless person repents, it is good for him. Repentance never goes to waste.

31
00:39:14,110 --> 00:39:33,650
Once a Companion had given bai'at at the hand of the Prophet ﷺ. After a while the Prophet ﷺ said: 'You too, give bai'at.' He said: 'O Messenger of Allah ﷺ, I have already given bai'at.' The Prophet ﷺ said: 'What of it? Give bai'at again.'

32
00:39:36,030 --> 00:39:42,290
SubhanAllah. 'What of it? Give bai'at again.' See how it is.

33
00:39:42,430 --> 00:39:53,630
Where else would you find this? It looks like a small book, but it is full of knowledge you won't find in big books — here it is, in a few pages.

34
00:39:56,050 --> 00:40:25,250
[He reads the passage again: repentance after bai'at, and the Companion told to give bai'at again.]

35
00:40:25,850 --> 00:40:38,830
Let me give you another example. I have studied hadith; I have read a great deal of hadith.

36
00:40:39,290 --> 00:40:58,950
Once it was the time of the Isha prayer. The Prophet ﷺ was sitting with his Companions. As the prayer began everyone stood up — but one of them stayed sitting.

37
00:41:01,670 --> 00:41:23,570
He said: 'I have already given bai'at.' 'What of it? Give bai'at again — otherwise it is of no use.'

38
00:41:26,030 --> 00:41:46,550
'Do not stay apart; give bai'at.' Do you understand? This is how it is: once you have given bai'at, renew it. That is what I do — having given bai'at once, I give it again.

39
00:41:47,570 --> 00:42:08,330
Is it necessary for every person to become a murid? The answer: whoever considers it necessary to live by the rules of the path and wants to fulfil them at a high level. If you do not consider it necessary, then deprivation is your lot.

40
00:42:08,330 --> 00:42:25,470
There is an old saying: 'Whoever has no pir, his pir is Shaytan.' Do you see? This is very important.

41
00:42:26,890 --> 00:42:35,430
And it is easy to see why: the murid has his master's hand over him, so Shaytan cannot touch him.

42
00:43:58,780 --> 00:44:20,920
Now I see something — that is a different matter. Qadeer Piya Sarkar fixed his eyes on mine and looked at me, and kept telling me many things. I held his gaze; I kept looking, I kept listening.

43
00:44:21,120 --> 00:44:41,060
For years — from childhood until I married and had children. I was in Warangal; I have two children, Azeem Baba and Khwaja Baba. In Warangal I used to go about with Hazrat.

44
00:44:46,580 --> 00:45:00,360
Does it go to waste? People were Muslims, but when they saw the Prophet ﷺ, what did they become? Sahabis — Companions.

45
00:45:03,060 --> 00:45:27,800
Now no wali can reach their rank, however great — because they looked at the Prophet ﷺ with their own eyes, and the Prophet ﷺ looked at them. Do you understand what being a Companion is?

46
00:45:28,580 --> 00:46:01,840
So what is happening? Through the eyes, the voice, the warmth, the breath — through so many things — sitting with a wali, what spreads from his body, his heart, his mind and his thoughts reaches you. It does not reach others; those who are with him are the ones affected.

47
00:46:01,840 --> 00:46:48,960
Is it necessary for everyone? Whoever hates bad things and keeps away from them, who is drawn to good things, wants to see and hear good and become good himself, and considers it necessary to keep the rules of the path at a high level — [for him it is]. If you do not consider it necessary, you are deprived.

48
00:46:49,900 --> 00:46:57,980
To those who raise objections, Hazrat gives this answer too: this is the saying of the elders.

49
00:47:06,400 --> 00:47:25,340
Someone may also say: the books of the Qur'an and hadith are available, the books of tasawwuf and Sunnah are available — then why did different ways arise: Qadri, Chishti, Naqshbandi and so on?

50
00:47:26,100 --> 00:48:12,820
The one who derives rulings from the Qur'an and hadith is a faqih, a jurist: from the verses of the Qur'an and the practice of the Prophet ﷺ he says, 'This is a matter of fiqh; act on it.' What is fiqh? Looking at the verses of the Qur'an and the hadith of the Prophet ﷺ: this is the ruling.

51
00:48:17,450 --> 00:48:43,350
The answer is: just as the jurists who derive rulings from the Qur'an and hadith differ — some Hanafi, some Shafi'i, some Maliki, some Hanbali — so too those who derive the path from the Qur'an and hadith are Qadri, Chishti, Naqshbandi and so on. MashaAllah, SubhanAllah.

52
00:48:44,790 --> 00:49:12,460
Now look: when we do wudu from the Qur'an, what do we do? After the washing we do masah — we wipe the head. In that we are Hanafi: we wipe a quarter of the head.

53
00:49:15,360 --> 00:49:41,720
Another school wipes the whole head — is it the Hanbali or the Shafi'i? Perhaps the Hanbali wipe the whole head, and the Shafi'i just a little. We wipe a quarter.

54
00:49:43,440 --> 00:49:59,000
This is fiqh. In the Qur'an, what is it? 'Wipe your heads' — whatever the words for it are — and one school does it this way, another that way.

55
00:50:00,100 --> 00:50:30,920
Those who studied the hadith and practised them understood the questions of fiqh and began to act on them; those who follow a particular way are different: Hanafi, Shafi'i, Hanbali — all of them are like this.

56
00:50:31,580 --> 00:50:49,020
Some, even in prayer, do it like this — raising the hands; it is called raf'-ul-yadain. The Hanafis do not do raf'-ul-yadain; the Shafi'is and Hanbalis do. See how far we have come.

57
00:50:50,120 --> 00:51:11,300
In the same way there are those who chose a path: Qadri, Chishti, Naqshbandi and so on. So what are we all? People who have chosen a path and are walking on it.

58
00:51:12,180 --> 00:52:07,180
Let me read another line. Remember one thing: people's temperaments differ; their natures are different. Some are drawn to one way and some to another; each finds his own way, and for each there is a way. Different families of the path have different ways.

59
00:52:36,840 --> 00:52:52,620
See, it is Allah's great grace: He gives to whom He wills. And so much knowledge [in so few pages].

